# React Rules & Patterns

Reference: [Rules of React](https://react.dev/reference/rules), [Thinking in React](https://react.dev/learn/thinking-in-react), [Keeping Components Pure](https://react.dev/learn/keeping-components-pure)

---

## Component Structure

```tsx
interface ResumeUploadProps {
  onUpload: (file: File) => void;
  isUploading?: boolean;
}

export function ResumeUpload({ onUpload, isUploading }: ResumeUploadProps) {
  return (/* ... */);
}
```

- **UI components**: Presentational only; receive data via props
- **Logic**: Custom hooks (`useLogin`, `useResumeUpload`) or services — not inside JSX
- **Hooks location**: `src/hooks/` or colocated in feature folders

---

## Rules of React

These are rules—not guidelines. Breaking them often causes bugs. Use Strict Mode and [eslint-plugin-react-hooks](https://www.npmjs.com/package/eslint-plugin-react-hooks).

### 1. Components and Hooks must be pure

- **Props and state are immutable** — Never mutate them. Use setters for state; pass new props down.
- **Side effects outside render** — No `fetch`, `document.title`, mutations, or non-idempotent calls (`new Date()`, `Math.random()`) during render. Use event handlers or `useEffect`.
- **Idempotent** — Same inputs (props, state, context) → same output. Avoid `new Date()` or `Math.random()` in render.
- **Local mutation is OK** — Mutating a variable created inside the component (e.g. building an array) is fine. Mutating values from outside (props, globals) is not.

```tsx
// ❌ Bad: side effect in render
function Page({ product }) {
  document.title = product.title;
  return <h1>{product.name}</h1>;
}

// ✅ Good: use useEffect
function Page({ product }) {
  useEffect(() => {
    document.title = product.title;
  }, [product.title]);
  return <h1>{product.name}</h1>;
}
```

### 2. React calls Components and Hooks

- **Never pass hooks as values** — Hooks only inside components.
- **Never call components as functions** — Use JSX: `<MyComponent />`, not `MyComponent()`.

### 3. Rules of Hooks

- **Only from React functions** — Call hooks from components or custom hooks, not from plain JS.
- **Only at top level** — No hooks inside loops, conditions, or nested functions. Call them before any early returns.

```tsx
// ❌ Bad
if (condition) {
  const [x, setX] = useState(0);
}

// ✅ Good
const [x, setX] = useState(0);
if (!condition) return null;
```

---

## Thinking in React (5 steps)

1. **Break UI into component hierarchy** — Map mockup to components; match data model shape.
2. **Build static version first** — No state; pass data via props. Top-down or bottom-up.
3. **Identify minimal state** — What changes? Where does it live? (Lift state up if needed.)
4. **Determine where state lives** — Single source of truth; often the nearest common parent.
5. **Add inverse data flow** — Callbacks from children to parents to update state.

---

## Purity checklist

| In render | Use instead |
|-----------|-------------|
| `fetch()` | `useEffect` or Server Component `await` |
| `document.title = x` | `useEffect` |
| `new Date()`, `Math.random()` | `useState` + `useEffect` or event handler |
| Mutating props/state | New values, setters |
| Mutating objects passed to JSX | Create new objects before passing |

